<title>Approximating a nonlinear function by a linear function</title>

<p><ref>Video</ref> | <ref>Summary</ref> | <ref>Transcript</ref>  </p>
<description>
The secant line and tangent line are two ways to approximate a nonlinear function by a linear one.
</description>

<section><title>Transcript: 
Approximating a nonlinear function by a linear function</title>
  
<p>A linear function is nice and easy to deal with. For example, if I have a function <m>f(x)</m> that’s linear, then we could look at the slope of this function. If we view the function as an <ref to="https://mathinsight.org/function_machine">input-output machine</ref>, then the slope is the change in the output over the change in the input. How do you determine the slope of the linear function? Well, let’s draw its graph, so let's plot the graph of <m>f</m> in blue. Let’s say we start at some input, we’ll call <m>x_0</m>, then its output is <m>f(x_0)</m>. Then let's change its input by amount, let's call it <m>\Delta x</m>, so that the new input is now <m>x_0 + \Delta x</m>. Well, then the output changes to <m>f(x_0 + \Delta x)</m>. How much did the input change? Well, it just changed by <m>\Delta x</m>. How much did the output change? Well, that's just equal to the new output, <m>f(x_0 + \Delta x)</m> minus the old output, <m>f(x_0)</m>. 
<me>\text{Change in output} = f(x_0 + \Delta x) - f(x_0)</me>
And we can call that change, <m>\Delta f</m>. 
Therefore we can put the slope, which we often denote by the letter <m>m</m>, as equal to <m>\frac{\Delta f}{\Delta x}</m>, or <m>f(x_0 + \Delta x) - f(x_0)</m> (that's the change in the output), divided by the change in the input, <m>\Delta x</m>
<me>\text{Slope} = m = \frac{\Delta f}{\Delta x} = \frac{f(x_0 + \Delta x) - f(x_0)}{\Delta x}.</me>
</p>
<p>Or maybe the notation would be easier if we labeled the new point as <m>x_1</m> -- and then the output at the new point would just be <m>f(x_1)</m>; so <m>x_1</m> is just another way to write <m>(x_0 + \Delta x)</m>. If we did that, then the slope would also be written as <m>f(x_1) - f(x_0)</m> (that's the change in the output). So the change in the output looks a little simpler, but now the input is going to be <m>x_1 - x_0</m>
<me>m =  \frac{f(x_1) - f(x_0)}{x_1 - x_0}.</me>
Both of these two expressions are the same thing, it just depends on whether you wanna call the second point <m>x_1</m>, or <m>(x_0 + \Delta x)</m>. For a linear function, the slope does not depend on the choice of the point <m>x_0</m>, nor the width of the interval <m>\Delta x</m>. That's an important property of a linear function, its slope is the same everywhere. The slope is equal to the rate of change of the linear function. And the rate of change of a linear function is always the same.</p> 
<p>Linear functions are nice and easy to deal with, but most of the time we'll be dealing with nonlinear functions. One part of calculus is approximating nonlinear functions by linear functions. How do we go about approximating nonlinear functions by linear functions? </p>
<p>Let’s imagine I have a nonlinear function, we’ll call it <m>f(x)</m> again. So if I want to approximate the function <m>f</m> by a linear function, I could pick two points, let’s call one <m>x_0</m> and one <m>x_1</m>. And I could draw a  line from the point on the graph of <m>f</m> above the point <m>x_0</m> to the point on the graph above the point <m>x_1</m>, it would look something like this. (<em>Key moment 1 - 3:38</em>) This line is an approximation to the function <m>f</m>, and it's called a secant line. The secant line is a line that goes through two points on the graph of the function. The slope of the secant line gives the average rate of change of <m>f</m>. What is the slope of the secant line? Well, it's the same expression we had before, for the linear function <m>f</m>. It was the change in output, <m>\Delta f</m>, which is the change in the heights of these two points, divided by the change in input, <m>\Delta x</m>, which is the distance between these two input points. In other words, it is <m>f(x_1) - f(x_0)</m> (that's the change in the output), divided by the change in the input, <m>x_1 - x_0</m>
<me>\text{Slope of secant} = \text{average rate of change of } f = \frac{\Delta f}{\Delta x} = \frac{f(x_1) - f(x_0)}{x_1 - x_0}.</me>
For a linear function <m>f</m>, the slope didn’t depend on the choice of the points, <m>x_0</m> and <m>x_1</m>. Does it matter now for our nonlinear function <m>f</m>? The slope of the secant line will depend on the choice of both the points. If I choose a different point for <m>x_0</m> and <m>x_1</m>, I get a different secant line whose slope could be much different. So, unlike for a linear function, this slope depends on the choice of the points. We can also write this formula in terms of <m>x_0</m> and <m>\Delta x</m>, rather than in terms of <m>x_1</m>, and this formula is exactly like the other formula we had for the linear function. The change in the input is <m>f(x_0 + \Delta x)</m> (that's the top point), minus <m>f(x_0)</m> divided by the change in the input (which is simply <m>\Delta x</m>)
<me>\text{Slope} = \frac{\Delta f}{\Delta x} = \frac{f(x_0 + \Delta x) - f(x_0)}{\Delta x}.</me></p>
<p>Now, these secant lines are nice linear approximations of the function <m>f</m>, but for many applications, we want to know what the slope of the function looks  like as we zoom in. If we zoom closely in here, we can see that the secant line (in red), is not the best approximation of the function <m>f</m>, which is graphed by the blue curve. How can we get the best approximation right near <m>x_0</m>? Well, we can’t just set <m>\Delta x</m> to zero. If we did that, we would get that <m>\frac{\Delta f}{\Delta x}</m> is <m>\frac{0}{0}</m>, and this is undefined. So that's not the way we can get the best linear approximation for the function <m>f</m> around the point <m>x_0</m>, but it's the right idea. We can make <m>\Delta x</m> smaller and smaller. The smaller we make <m>\Delta x</m>, the better the approximation right near <m>x_0</m>. As long as we don’t make <m>\Delta x</m> all the way 0, so that we run into the problem with this undefined slope, we’ll get better and better approximations the smaller we make <m>\Delta x</m>. 
(<em>Key moment 2 - 6:41</em>) We can use the slope of the secant line applet, to explore how a secant line depends on the value of <m>x_0</m>, as well as the interval <m>\Delta x</m>. This dark blue point is at the coordinates <m>(x_0, f(x_0))</m>. If we add <m>\Delta x</m> to the <m>x</m> coordinate, we get to the cyan point whose coordinates are <m>(x_0 + \Delta x, f(x_0 + \Delta x))</m>. 
Right now, <m>x_0</m> is set at 0.3, and <m>\Delta x</m> is 1. In this case, <m>\Delta f</m> (or this applet calls it <m>\Delta y</m> because it's the <m>y</m>-coordinate) happens to be 1.6, as you can see from this calculation right here. So the change in the input is 1, and the change in the output is 1.6. The slope is therefore 1.6. Now let's imagine we wanted to have a linear approximation to <m>f</m>, the curve in green, that is a really good approximation to <m>f</m>, right in the neighborhood of this blue point. To improve this approximation we can shrink the value of <m>\Delta x</m>. We could make it 0.1, rather than 1, or even make it 0.01. In fact we can zoom in, and we can see indeed that the red line is close to the green curve. In this case the slope of the secant line is 0.61. We can make <m>\Delta x</m> even smaller. If we make it 0.001, now we see that the slope is 0.601. If we make it even smaller, well, now it's 0.600 (there’s probably another 1 here, but it's rounded off). So it looks like the slope is getting very close to the value 0.6, as we make <m>\Delta x</m> smaller and smaller. But of course we can’t exactly make <m>\Delta x</m> zero, if we do that we get <m>\frac{0}{0}</m> as we said before – that's undefined. Instead, what we can do is put in a really really small in for <m>\Delta x</m>, to see that we’re very close to 0.6.</p>
<p>For the function we saw in the applet, the slope of the secant given by this formula, seemed to approach a given number as we made this distance <m>\Delta x</m> get smaller and smaller. In fact, it looked like the secant lines were approaching a particular line, as <m>\Delta x</m> got closer and closer to 0. This particular line is called the tangent line, and the value that the slope <m>\frac{\Delta f}{\Delta x}</m> is approaching, as <m>\Delta x</m> gets closer and closer to 0, is called the limit of <m>\frac{\Delta f}{\Delta x}</m> as <m>\Delta x</m> approaches 0. We write this as 
<me>\lim _{\Delta x \to 0} \frac{\Delta f}{\Delta x}</me>
which , by this formula, is the limit as <m>\Delta x</m> goes to zero, of <m>f(x_0+\Delta x) - f(x_0)</m> over <m>\Delta x</m>
<me>\lim_{\Delta x \to 0} \frac{f(x_0+\Delta x) - f(x_0)}{\Delta x}.</me>
</p>

<p>Here we’re not defining a limit precisely, but it's just the value that this quantity gets closer to, as <m>\Delta x</m> gets closer and closer to zero. Course we can’t let <m>\Delta x</m> get exactly to zero, because then we would have <m>\frac{0}{0}</m>, but we can let it get really really close. We can think of the idea of asking a two year old not to touch a lamp. What will the two year old do? He will look at you, and try to get as close as he can to touching the lamp without actually touching it. He’ll see how close he can get without getting into trouble. That’s the idea here. We get into trouble if we let  <m>\Delta x</m> be exactly zero, but we’ll get as close as we possibly can.  (<em>Key moment 3 - 8:57</em>) Remember that the slope of the secant line was the average rate of change of <m>f</m>. On the other hand the slope of the tangent line, we can refer to as the instantaneous rate of change. And we’ll denote it not by <m>\frac{\Delta f}{\Delta x}</m>, but instead as <m>\frac{df}{dx}</m>, and by this we just mean the limit, which we rewrite over here
<me>\frac{df}{dx} = \lim_{\Delta x \to 0} \frac{\Delta f}{\Delta x} = \lim_{\Delta x \to 0} \frac{f(x_0 + \Delta x)- f(x_0)}{\Delta x}.</me> 
So again, the line with the slope is called the tangent line, and the slope itself is called the derivative of <m>f</m> at the point where <m>x</m> is equal to <m>x_0</m>. To emphasize that the slope is calculated at the particular point, we often write it as <m>\frac{df}{dx}</m> evaluated at <m>x = x_0</m>: 
<me>\frac{df}{dx} \biggr\rvert_{x=x_0}</me> 
or sometimes we write it with a different notation, called <m>f</m> "prime" at <m>x_0</m>
<me>f’(x_0).</me></p>
<p>Either of these ways denote the derivative. The derivative is the slope of the tangent line and we often say it is the slope of the function itself at <m>x = x_0</m>. </p>
  
<p>(<em>Key moment 4 - 12:08</em>) In the secant line applet, remember if we put in <m>\Delta x = 0</m>, the slope was undefined. However, if we check this checkbox “show limit for <m>\Delta x = 0</m>”, when we plug in <m>\Delta x = 0</m>, the applet shows the limit; the derivative as <m>\Delta x</m> goes to zero. And indeed, we see in the case when <m>x_0 = 0.3</m>, that the derivative is 0.6. Of course this derivative depends on the value of <m>x_0</m>. If we change <m>x_0</m>, the slope changes. In other words, the derivative takes on different values depending on the value of the point at which you evaluate the derivative. 
</p>
</section>
